            <ArticleTitle>The Power of Connecting Dots: Advanced Techniques to Evaluate Brain Functional Connectivity in Humans.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1109/TBME.2016.2621727</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Brain functional connectivity estimation allows to depict patterns of cerebral activity not understandable otherwise with the standard brain imaging techniques such as functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) as well as electro or magnetoencephalography (hr-EEG, MEG). This special issue of TBME reports a range of methodological innovations toward the estimation of functional connectivity from brain activity data, with emphasis on neuroelectric and hemodynamic imaging modalities. Functional connectivity methodologies enable &quot;connecting of the dots&quot; derived from brain activity observations over multiple distributed sites, as depicted by such fMRI and hr-EEG/MEG devices.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
